[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi,</p>\n<p>I'm currently trying to formalize some consequences of the Compactness Theorem and have been running into some issues:</p>\n<p>I'm trying to quantify over all variables of a formula </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"TeX\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">\\phi</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>with free variables </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"TeX\"><pre><span></span><code>x<span class=\"nb\">_</span>1, <span class=\"k\">\\dots</span>, x<span class=\"nb\">_</span>n\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>If I understand the implementation in mathlib correctly, </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"TeX\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"k\">\\phi</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>should have type </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Formula</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">),</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>where L is the language I'm working in. I have found the definition</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">FirstOrder</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Language</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormula</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">alls</span><span class=\"o\">,</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>which seems to do something similar to what I want, but turns bounded formulas into formulas, while I'm trying to turn a formula into a sentence. Unfortunately, I have not quite understood the implementation details of </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">FirstOrder</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Language</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">BoundedFormula</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>yet.</p>",
        "id": 590875091,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruth Plümer",
        "timestamp": 1777125828
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Perhaps \\phi should be a <code>L.BoundedFormula Empty n</code> instead? the docs <br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=FirstOrder.Language.BoundedFormula#doc\">docs#FirstOrder.Language.BoundedFormula</a> suggest that means no free variables and n bound variables</p>",
        "id": 590879355,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777128552
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can quantify over all variables of <code>φ : L.Formula (Fin n)</code> and turn it into a sentence just using <code>(φ.relabel Sum.inr).alls : L.Sentence</code> to convert free variables to bound variables, then applying <code>alls</code> to universally quantify over all bound variables. This will give yo a formula with no free variables (<code>L.Formula Empty</code>), which is a sentence (<code>L.Sentence</code>).<br>\nYou may also use <code>Formula.iAlls</code>which does the same internally</p>",
        "id": 590879435,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Cipollina",
        "timestamp": 1777128626
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>you may also be interested in comparing it with <a href=\"https://github.com/FormalizedFormalLogic/Foundation/blob/master/Foundation/Syntax/Predicate/Quantifier.lean\">this</a>  alternative development which may result more intuitive since it works without relabeling functions and uses Pi and Sigma for existential and universal quantifiers.</p>",
        "id": 590889097,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Cipollina",
        "timestamp": 1777136165
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks, but I'm planning to eventually contribute to mathlib with my code, so that is unfortunately not a solution for me. But your explanation was very helpful!</p>",
        "id": 590894119,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruth Plümer",
        "timestamp": 1777139721
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"779253\">@Matteo Cipollina</span> Unfortunately, your suggestion does not seem to work. If I write </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">relabel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sum</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">alls</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sentence</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>in Lean, Lean complains because </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">relabel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sum</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inr</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">alls</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>has type </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Formula</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">?</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">53</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⊕</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>but should have type</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Formula</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Any ideas how to fix this?</p>",
        "id": 591096347,
        "sender_full_name": "Ruth Plümer",
        "timestamp": 1777285613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"729643\">@Ruth Plümer</span>  Indeed BoundedFormula.alls works over bound variables in a scope, while Formula.iAlls handles these under the hood. The below mwe may help you here:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ModelTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Syntax</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ModelTheory</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Semantics</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">open</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">FirstOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Language</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Classical</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">variable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Language</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- Example: Create a formula with n free variables</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Formula</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">L</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Sentence</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"c1\">-- First relabel to Empty ⊕ Fin n, then apply iAlls</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Formula</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">relabel</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Sum</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">inr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">φ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">iAlls</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 591111873,
        "sender_full_name": "Matteo Cipollina",
        "timestamp": 1777290174
    }
